        "id": "4a4b9f60-7ff5-433d-bbe6-57f556c21fd3",
        "title": "Understanding Antibiotic Resistance & Proper Dosage Adherence",
        "slug": "understanding-antibiotic-resistance",
        "content": "<h2>What is Antibiotic Resistance?</h2>\n<p>Antibiotic resistance occurs when bacteria evolve to survive exposure to antibiotics that previously killed them. This is one of the most pressing public health threats globally.</p>\n\n<h2>Why Complete Your Course?</h2>\n<p>Even if you feel better after a few days, stopping antibiotics early can allow surviving bacteria to develop resistance. Always complete the full prescribed course.</p>\n\n<h2>Common Mistakes to Avoid</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Stopping medication when symptoms improve</li>\n<li>Sharing antibiotics with others</li>\n<li>Using leftover antibiotics for new illnesses</li>\n<li>Not following dosage instructions</li>\n<li>Demanding antibiotics for viral infections</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>What You Can Do</h2>\n<p>Only take antibiotics prescribed by a qualified healthcare professional. Never demand antibiotics for viral infections like the common cold or flu.</p>\n\n<h2>How Jumarald Helps</h2>\n<p>Our pharmacists provide clear dosage instructions with every prescription. We also offer medication reminders through WhatsApp to help you stay on track.</p>",
        "summary": "Why completing your prescribed course of antibiotics is essential to prevent bacterial mutation and treatment failure.",
        "author": "Dr. Chioma Nwachukwu, PharmD",
        "imageUrl": "https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1584308666744-24d5c474f2ae?w=800&q=80",
        "tags": [
          "Antibiotics",
          "Health Tips",
          "Medication Safety"
        ],
        "category": null,
        "isPublished": true,
        "viewCount": 0,
        "createdAt": "2026-08-06T15:45:19.527Z",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-06T15:45:19.527Z"
      },
      {
        "id": "91d6ade3-2f39-4a92-9ac7-c2eb70383923",
        "title": "Managing Diabetes in Ghana: A Complete Guide",
        "slug": "managing-diabetes-ghana",
        "content": "<h2>Diabetes in Ghana</h2>\n<p>Diabetes affects approximately 1 million Ghanaians, with Type 2 diabetes being the most common form. Proper management is crucial to prevent complications.</p>\n\n<h2>Understanding Blood Sugar Levels</h2>\n<p>Normal fasting blood sugar is below 5.6 mmol/L. Pre-diabetes is 5.6-6.9 mmol/L, and diabetes is 7.0 mmol/L or higher.</p>\n\n<h2>Essential Management Tools</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Blood glucose monitor - Check levels regularly</li>\n<li>Test strips - Keep a steady supply</li>\n<li>Insulin (if prescribed) - Proper storage is critical</li>\n<li>Medication - Take as prescribed</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>Diet Tips for Ghanaians</h2>\n<p>Reduce intake of white rice, white bread, and sugary drinks. Replace with brown rice, whole grain bread, and water. Portion control is key.</p>\n\n<h2>Cold Chain Storage</h2>\n<p>Insulin must be stored between 2°C-8°C. Jumarald ensures cold chain delivery for all insulin products across Greater Accra.</p>",
        "summary": "Comprehensive guide to managing diabetes in Ghana, including blood sugar monitoring, diet tips, and cold-chain insulin storage.",
        "author": "Dr. Adebayo Ogunlesi, MD",
        "imageUrl": "https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1471864190281-a93a3070b6de?w=800&q=80",
        "tags": [
          "Diabetes",
          "Chronic Disease",
          "Health Tips"
        ],
        "category": null,
        "isPublished": true,
        "viewCount": 0,
        "createdAt": "2026-08-06T15:45:19.993Z",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-06T15:45:19.993Z"
      },
      {
        "id": "c9a626d8-bc99-4ebb-82ea-ce2a53c2aab9",
        "title": "Malaria Prevention: What Every Family in Prampram Should Know",
        "slug": "malaria-prevention-prampram",
        "content": "<h2>Malaria in Ghana</h2>\n<p>Malaria remains the leading cause of outpatient visits in Ghana. Children under 5 and pregnant women are most vulnerable.</p>\n\n<h2>Prevention is Better Than Cure</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Use insecticide-treated mosquito nets (ITNs) every night</li>\n<li>Apply insect repellent containing DEET</li>\n<li>Wear long sleeves and trousers at dusk and dawn</li>\n<li>Clear stagnant water around your home</li>\n<li>Use window and door screens</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>Recognizing Malaria Symptoms</h2>\n<p>Fever, chills, headache, vomiting, and body aches are common symptoms. If fever persists for more than 2 days, seek medical attention immediately.</p>\n\n<h2>Treatment Options</h2>\n<p>Artemether-Lumefantrine (Coartem) is the first-line treatment for uncomplicated malaria. Available without prescription at Jumarald Pharmacy.</p>\n\n<h2>When to Seek Emergency Care</h2>\n<p>Severe malaria can be fatal. Go to the hospital immediately if you experience: high fever, convulsions, difficulty breathing, or severe weakness.</p>",
        "summary": "Essential malaria prevention strategies and treatment options for families in Prampram and Greater Accra.",
        "author": "Philip Bruce-Tagoe, Superintendent Pharmacist",
        "imageUrl": "https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1584036561566-baf8f5f1b144?w=800&q=80",
        "tags": [
          "Malaria",
          "Prevention",
          "Family Health"
        ],
        "category": null,
        "isPublished": true,
        "viewCount": 0,
        "createdAt": "2026-08-06T15:45:20.302Z",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-06T15:45:20.302Z"
      },
      {
        "id": "faed4ddf-8546-469c-ae7d-aff5196e2de6",
        "title": "Hypertension: The Silent Killer Affecting Ghanaian Adults",
        "slug": "hypertension-silent-killer",
        "content": "<h2>The Hidden Danger</h2>\n<p>Hypertension affects approximately 1 in 3 adults in Ghana, yet many don't know they have it. It's called the \"silent killer\" because it often has no symptoms.</p>\n\n<h2>What is Normal Blood Pressure?</h2>\n<p>Normal: Below 120/80 mmHg<br/>Elevated: 120-129/80 mmHg<br/>High (Stage 1): 130-139/80-89 mmHg<br/>High (Stage 2): 140+/90+ mmHg</p>\n\n<h2>Lifestyle Changes That Help</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Reduce salt intake (max 5g per day)</li>\n<li>Exercise 30 minutes daily</li>\n<li>Maintain healthy weight (BMI 18.5-24.9)</li>\n<li>Limit alcohol consumption</li>\n<li>Quit smoking</li>\n<li>Manage stress</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>Medication Adherence</h2>\n<p>If prescribed blood pressure medication, take it every day as directed. Missing doses can cause dangerous spikes in blood pressure.</p>\n\n<h2>Free Blood Pressure Checks</h2>\n<p>Jumarald Pharmacy offers free blood pressure monitoring every Saturday from 9AM-12PM at our Prampram location.</p>",
        "summary": "Understanding hypertension, its risks, and how to manage it through lifestyle changes and medication adherence.",
        "author": "Dr. Kofi Mensah, Cardiologist",
        "imageUrl": "https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1559757175-5700dde675bc?w=800&q=80",
        "tags": [
          "Hypertension",
          "Heart Health",
          "Prevention"
        ],
        "category": null,
        "isPublished": true,
        "viewCount": 0,
        "createdAt": "2026-08-06T15:45:20.613Z",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-06T15:45:20.613Z"
      },
      {
        "id": "8c89c4b9-5a52-439b-9384-986de0334a3f",
        "title": "Child Health: Vaccination Schedule Every Parent Should Follow",
        "slug": "child-health-vaccination-schedule",
        "content": "<h2>Why Vaccinations Matter</h2>\n<p>Vaccinations protect children from serious diseases like measles, polio, and meningitis. Ghana's Expanded Programme on Immunization (EPI) provides free vaccines at health centers.</p>\n\n<h2>Ghana's Vaccination Schedule</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Birth: BCG (Tuberculosis), OPV 0, Hepatitis B 0</li>\n<li>6 weeks: OPV 1, Penta 1, PCV 1, Rotavirus 1</li>\n<li>10 weeks: OPV 2, Penta 2, PCV 2, Rotavirus 2</li>\n<li>14 weeks: OPV 3, Penta 3, PCV 3, Rotavirus 3</li>\n<li>9 months: Measles-Rubella 1, Yellow Fever</li>\n<li>15 months: Measles-Rubella 2, Meningitis A</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>Common Concerns</h2>\n<p>Mild fever after vaccination is normal. Give paracetamol as directed by your healthcare provider. Keep the child hydrated and monitor for 48 hours.</p>\n\n<h2>What Jumarald Offers</h2>\n<p>We stock pediatric formulations of paracetamol, ORS, and children's vitamins to support your child's health alongside vaccinations.</p>",
        "summary": "Complete guide to Ghana's childhood vaccination schedule and how to support your child's immune health.",
        "author": "Dr. Amina Hassan, Pediatrician",
        "imageUrl": "https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1612277795421-9bc7706a4a34?w=800&q=80",
        "tags": [
          "Child Health",
          "Vaccination",
          "Prevention"
        ],
        "category": null,
        "isPublished": true,
        "viewCount": 0,
        "createdAt": "2026-08-06T15:45:20.924Z",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-06T15:45:20.924Z"
      }
